]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/153544"} "23 from 23 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Two laps of the track, or 920 metres."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The 1947 World Champion was Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/14671"} "Reg Harris"] " and he was considered a heavy favorite in this event, especially in the British press. The " [:em {} "Manchester Guardian"] " wrote that he “may well be regarded as almost certain to carry off the Olympic title.” He was helped by the fact that the rider he defeated in the 1947 World final was Holland’s Cor Bijster, who had turned professional in late 1947."] "\n\n" [:p {} "For the first time, the match sprint was conducted as best two of three races in phases besides the final – from the quarter-finals onwards. All the quarter- and semi-finals were decided in two straight races. Harris defeated Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/13540"} "Charlie Bazzano"] " in one semi, while Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/14479"} "Mario Ghella"] " swept Denmark’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/16471"} "Axel Schandorff"] " in the other semi. In the second race of his semi, Ghella had shown his potential with an 11.9 finish, the fastest time of any race in London."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final was not close, but it was not what the British expected. In the first race, Ghella rode away to a five-length victory, when Harris made a tactical error leaving room for the Italian to pass on the inside. Harris eased up when he was beaten, saving himself for the next two races, but that would not happen. The second race saw the same result, the younger Italian easily winning by two lengths to take the gold medal. Schandorff won the bronze by defeating Bazzano in two straight races."] "\n\n" [:p {} "A few weeks later, Ghella won the World Championship in the sprint, defeating Schandorff in the final, with Harris placing third, defeating his Olympic tandem partner, " [:a {:href "/athletes/13498"} "Alan Bannister"] ". Harris turned professional and won the World Sprint title in 1949, the first time that was ever won by a " [:em {} "néo-pro"] ". He would defend that title in 1950 and 1951 and won again in 1954, and would place second in 1956. Amazingly, in 1974, he came out of retirement and won the British sprint championship."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "R1 Rep"] [:th {} "R2"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/14479"} "Mario Ghella"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "12.9 (1 h11)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "12.0 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/14671"} "Reg Harris"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "14.4 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "15.1 (1 h8)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "0 (2 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/16471"} "Axel Schandorff"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/DEN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/DEN.png"}] "DEN"]] [:td {} "12.5 (1 h7)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "12.5 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "0 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:h